## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to translate product goals into a financially grounded technical plan, balancing on-device ML costs, user privacy, and rollout risk.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML cost modeling (energy, memory, inference latency)
- Data transfer savings and network impact
- Privacy controls and regulatory compliance
- OTA update cadence and maintenance costs
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost model for CBA calculations
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.inferences * params.energyPerInference;
  const memory = params.modelSize + params.cacheSize;
  const dataSavings = params.uploadSize * params.reductionRate;
  return { energy, memory, dataSavings };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cost-benefit framework for cross-app, on-device data sharing with strong privacy constraints and a realistic rollout plan.

## Key Concepts

- On-device cross-app data sharing with strict isolation
- User opt-out and privacy/compliance controls
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, energy
- Telemetry delta economics and governance
- Staged rollout and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: anonymize and bucket signals before local merge
function processEvent(event) {
  if (!userOptIn) return;
  const bucket = hash(event.type) % 10;
  buckets[bucket].push(event);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and adapt to schema drift in shared signals?
- What rollback plan and telemetry safeguards would you implement if opt-out is toggled mid-rollout?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a lightweight edge optimization with clear cost/benefit trade-offs, focusing on data movement, energy, and privacy in mobile environments.

## Key Concepts

- Content-based fingerprinting
- Bloom filters for on-device dedup
- Bandwidth vs CPU energy trade-offs
- Privacy considerations and optional cloud verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute hash and check Bloom filter
function canSend(record, bloom) {
  const h = hash(record); // deterministic fingerprint
  if (bloom.mightContain(h)) return false;
  bloom.add(h);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate Bloom filter false positive rate given device memory constraints?
- How would you rollout to mitigate drift in data patterns and ensure continued value?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a pragmatic CBA for on-device decision logic that changes user experience while preserving privacy and reducing server load. Tests modeling of energy, storage, and regulatory costs alongside rollout governance.

## Key Concepts

- On-device decisioning and privacy
- Compute and energy modeling
- Data minimization and consent logging
- Telemetry footprint and storage planning
- Rollout gates and break-even analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo cost model for CBA
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.cpuW * params.hours;
  const storage = params.rulesKB * params.devices / 1024;
  const savings = params.backendCostPerUnit * params.throughputReduction;
  const compliance = params.complianceCost;
  return energy + storage - savings + compliance;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate energy estimates across device SKUs?
- How would you adapt the model for regional regulatory differences?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical CBA for a local-first search feature, balancing storage, CPU, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing
- Delta sync costs
- Privacy/compliance
- Update cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function indexRepo(files) {
  // naive tokenization
  const index = {};
  for (const f of files) {
    const tokens = f.content.split(/\\W+/).filter(Boolean);
    tokens.forEach(t => (index[t] ||= []).push(f.path));
  }
  return index;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure user time saved and energy impact to validate the break-even?
- How would you design for monorepos and very large repos with incremental indexing?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to translate a complex edge-ML rollout into a precise 2-year CBA, balancing hardware, cloud costs, and real-world benefits like downtime and maintenance savings.

## Key Concepts

- Edge computing and on-device training
- Federated learning and privacy implications
- CAPEX vs OPEX modeling
- Downtime reduction and MTBF uplift as financial drivers
- Handling non-IID data and drift in multi-site deployments

## Code Example

```javascript
function estimateROI(capex, opexPerMonth, savingsPerMonth, horizonMonths) {
  const totalOpex = opexPerMonth * horizonMonths;
  const totalCost = capex + totalOpex;
  const totalSavings = savingsPerMonth * horizonMonths;
  const roi = (totalSavings - totalCost) / totalCost;
  return { totalCost, totalSavings, roi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor and mitigate model drift across sites?
- What metrics would you track to validate the ROI assumptions and trigger governance gates?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about battery UX, scheduling, and real-world validation with a beginner feature.

## Key Concepts
- On-device scheduling
- Battery/CPU energy estimation
- Telemetry and privacy constraints
- Rollout strategy and break-even analysis
- UX impact

## Code Example
```javascript
function shouldBatch(now, lastSent, urgency, dnd) {
  if (urgency === 'urgent') return false;
  if (dnd) return true;
  const withinWindow = (now - lastSent) < 15*60*1000;
  return withinWindow;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate energy savings in production?
- How would you handle edge cases like user toggling DND mid-window?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a feature into a measurable business and engineering plan, focusing on CBA fundamentals, trade-offs, and governance for a client-side offline feature.

## Key Concepts

- **Client-side indexing and offline user experience**: Implementing lightweight search indexes (e.g., Lunr) for instant offline note search
- **Cost modeling**: Storage, CPU, latency, and privacy considerations
- **Rollout planning**: Phased deployment with validation metrics and maintenance scheduling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple Lunr index search
const index = lunr(function() {
  this.field('title', { boost: 10 });
  this.field('content');
  this.ref('id');
});

documents.forEach(doc => index.add(doc));

const results = index.search('search term');
```

## Why This Is Asked

Explore data-locality, privacy, and practical CBA modeling for a lightweight client-side feature in a cloud data platform.

## Key Concepts

- Data locality and offline caching
- Cost modeling: storage, compute, network
- Privacy/compliance: anonymization, retention, audits
- Rollout strategy and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
function estimateCBA(params) {
  // simple cost model placeholder
  const { storagePerUserMB, months, users } = params;
  const storageCostPerMB = 0.02; // hypothetical
  const totalStorage = storagePerUserMB * users * months;
  return totalStorage * storageCostPerMB;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust the model for enterprise vs SMB customers?
- What monitoring would you set to validate the forecast post-launch?
